course: dictionary
lesson: 10806
title: Various: several different kinds
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word various tells you there are several different things, but it does not say exactly how many. Alice says Dinah will «eat a little bird as soon as look at it», and the birds leave on various pretexts. Pretexts are made-up excuses, so the birds have several different reasons for going.
explain: Various is useful when you list kinds of things: various animals, various shapes, various kinds of rock. You can also say for various reasons, at various times, or in various ways. Each time, it means a number of different ones.
explain: In maths, be careful. Various gives no number, so you must find the number somewhere else in the question. The Mole «trudged off to the nearest village for milk and eggs and various necessaries»: several different things, but the story does not say how many.
fact: Various means several different, or more than one separate one.
fact: Various does not tell you how many, so it is not a number.
fact: «Various pretexts» means several made-up excuses.
fact: «Various necessaries» means several things you need, such as food and drink.
fact: In maths, look elsewhere in the question for the actual number.
exercise: Alice says the birds leave on «various pretexts». What are pretexts? | *Made-up excuses | Large, hungry birds | Long, sharp feathers | Rules about flying
exercise: The Mole goes to the village for «various necessaries». What does various mean here? | Only one thing | Old and dusty | Exactly ten things | *Several different things
exercise: Maths problem: «Sam buys various pencils for the class.» Where do you find how many pencils? | In the word various, because it is a number | In the word pencils | *In the number given elsewhere in the question | Nowhere, so the answer is always five
test: Which phrase means at different times? | at one time | at no time | *at various times | at the same time
test: Mia says various means exactly three. Is she right? | *No, various does not give a number | Yes, it always means three | Yes, but only in maths | Only in stories, never in maths
exam: Which sentence uses various correctly? | *We saw various birds in the park. | We saw birds, various. | Various the park birds saw. | We saw various, exactly two, birds in the park.
